t works great on AWS  but in order to get that into GCP we  have to route through the interconnects  to resolve all your dns connections and  that's a mess so we added up caching  that on the GCP side and we're looking  into a more like DNS mesh solution where  we have some Google clouds parts and  some AWS parts and they like mesh  together so that they have multiple  sources of truth for the DNS so external  DNS won't solve all of that together but  at least have helped you provide your  provision your private DNS so  the the benefit of this solution is that  all three of those components can be set  together with kubernetes configurations  so this is self-service to the user the  user can say I want private DNS or I'm  sorry I want private ingress but I want  public ingress and I configure that the  same time I deploy my application which  means that I as app as owner or a  cluster operator don't need to care  about how people are doing ingress I  provide some solutions and they opt into  them all these logs are available to  them and they can opt into them with the  annotation it's pretty slick so we do  have some operational challenges as well  when you are in multiple clusters  whether that's multiple environments or  multi-tenancy  we have to figure out whether you want  to aggregate or separate your metrics  and your logs so by default gke logs and  metrics go to stackdriver in the same  GCP project we didn't necessarily like  that very much so what that meant is we  had to give you permission to all of the  users on our shared clusters and we  didn't want to give them rate  permissions because they were our paths  projects and they would step on each  other's toes  so what that meant is we had to sort of  invent a solution that would emit  metrics or sorry logs to other projects  so we have a stack driver fluency  solution that will emit a duplicate of  the logs to the projects that they own  so that they can do log to metric  conversion and they can also do log  dashboards or metric log metric  dashboards for logging we mostly just  use stack driver for tracing we use data  dog like I mentioned for uptime checks  we use run run scope so one of the  benefits of run scope is that unlike  most things like Pingdom or other  external SAS providers that will do  checks of your applications run scope  allows you to deploy an agent behind  your firewall so we can do this in the  private network and it does a check of  your application so I'll say go talk a  little bit more about run scope in a  minute but about availability we also  provide a couple features of the paths  that don't come native which e ke that  includes  backups with ARC which I believe has a  new name that I forget we also deploy a  D scheduler which makes things evenly  deploy across the cloud and like was  mentioned note pool can be upgraded with  a click of the button on gke  unfortunately that only upgrades the OS  and the kubernetes version so if you  want to change the instance type or you  want to change node tags you have to do  your own recreation of the node pool so  we ended up doing was terraforming in a  Bluegreen deployment of node pools which  is a little complicated but it allows us  to do zero downtime deployments we have  to have like double the quota we  normally run which is kind of a hazard  but it works so a little bit about run  scope we deploy the agents on GCE and  also on gke we also wrote an operator  for this which again will probably be  open-source soon and this operator takes  currently ingress annotations so these  ingress annotations tell what endpoint  do I want to hit what back-end bucket do  I want to put them in and run scope and  then run scope will tell the agent or  rather the agent asks the the SAS  product like what should I check and  then the agent checks your applications  sometimes that's going horizontally on  the cluster  sometimes that's going out of the  networking back in the ingress path  sometimes that's 